# exitscreen schedule — install with:
#   ( crontab -l 2>/dev/null | grep -v 'exitscreen/run.py' ; cat deploy/crontab ) | crontab -
#
# Every run fetches and renders; run.py pushes to the panel only when the
# rendered image differs from the last one pushed. So a 5-minute cadence costs
# almost nothing: on a quiet afternoon most runs end in "no change".
#
# Why 5 minutes rather than 10, when the metro feed is only polled every 10:
# a delayed train changes the frame as soon as OVapi knows about it, rather than
# waiting for a fixed cycle. The guard makes the extra runs free.

# Render every 5 minutes while anyone is awake (07:00–21:55).
*/5 7-21 * * * cd /home/exitscreen/exitscreen && /home/exitscreen/venv/bin/python run.py >> /home/exitscreen/exitscreen.log 2>&1

# One more at 22:00 to close the window, since */5 7-21 stops at 21:55.
0 22 * * * cd /home/exitscreen/exitscreen && /home/exitscreen/venv/bin/python run.py >> /home/exitscreen/exitscreen.log 2>&1

# 06:59 — daily ghost-clear, then an immediate forced redraw. Clearing leaves the
# panel white, so --clear implies --force; otherwise the guard would see an
# unchanged image, skip the push, and leave the display blank all day.
59 6 * * * cd /home/exitscreen/exitscreen && /home/exitscreen/venv/bin/python run.py --clear >> /home/exitscreen/exitscreen.log 2>&1

# After a reboot: wait for NTP before drawing. This Pi has no RTC, so at boot it
# believes it is some time in the past — every departure would look "already
# gone" and get filtered out, leaving an empty metro column.
@reboot cd /home/exitscreen/exitscreen && /home/exitscreen/venv/bin/python run.py --wait-for-clock --force >> /home/exitscreen/exitscreen.log 2>&1
